    - How to pass props to class based component.
    - How to use state variable in class based component.
    - Life cycle of React 
        - First constructor will be call.
        - Then render the component
        - If parent component has child component then it's child constructor will be called.
        - child render the component
        - child componentDiMount() method will call.
        - At last parent componentDidMount() method will call.

    - componentDidMount() : This method is to make an API call.
    - Life cyle of react : https://projects.wojtekmaj.pl/react-lifecycle-methods-diagram/
        - React has two phases.
            1. Render phase : constructor and render will be call.
            2. Commit phase : change

        - if two child components called from the parent class then :
            - Parent Constructor call
            - Parent render the component
                - First Child constructor call
                - first child render the component
                
                - second child constructor call
                - second child render the component

                - first child componentDidMount() method will be call.
                - second child componentDidMount() method will be call.

            - Parent componentDidMount() method will be call.

    - componentDidMount() :
        - componentDidMount() is a lifecycle method in class components.
        - It runs once, immediately after the component is mounted (inserted into the DOM).
        - useCases :
            - Fetch data from an API
            - Start timers or subscriptions
            - Initialize third-party libraries
            - Manipulate the DOM (if necessary)
        Notes : Using the useEffect() hook with an empty dependency array ([]) mimics componentDidMount.
        Example :
                useEffect(() => {
                    // This runs only once after the first render
                    fetch("https://api.github.com/users/octocat")
                    .then(res => res.json())
                    .then(data => setUserData(data));
                }, []); // ← Empty array means "run only on mount"

    - componentDidUpdate() : 
        - componentDidUpdate() is a class component lifecycle method that runs after the component updates, i.e., after state or props change and the component re-renders.
        
        - Example :
            componentDidUpdate(prevProps, prevState) 
            {
                if (prevState.count !== this.state.count) 
                {
                    console.log("Count updated:", this.state.count);
                }
            }

        - In functional components, we use useEffect() with a dependency array to mimic componentDidUpdate.
        - Example :
            useEffect(() => {
                    console.log("Count updated:", count);
                }, [count]); // runs every time "count" changes

            Note :
                "componentDidMount runs once after the initial render, while componentDidUpdate runs after every re-render caused by changes in state or props."

    - componentWillUnmount() : 
        - componentWillUnmount() is a React class component lifecycle method that is called just before a component is removed from the DOM (i.e., unmounted or destroyed).
        - It is used mainly for cleanup tasks to prevent memory leaks or unwanted background processes.
        - Common Use Cases:
            - Clearing timers or intervals
            - Removing event listeners
        Note : 
            - In functional components, you achieve the same behavior using a cleanup function inside useEffect().
            - Example :
                  useEffect(() => {
                        const interval = setInterval(() => {
                        setSeconds((prev) => prev + 1);
                        }, 1000);

                        // cleanup function (called on unmount)
                        return () => {
                        clearInterval(interval);
                        console.log("Timer component removed from DOM");
                        };
                    }, []); // empty dependency -> runs once

